\description{
The Generalised Ex-Gaussian of Marmolejo-Ramos et al. (2023): the ex-Gaussian
with one extra \code{shape} parameter, obtained by raising its CDF to a power.

\deqn{F_{GEG}(x) = \left[F_{EG}(x)\right]^{shape}}

so that the density is

\deqn{f_{GEG}(x) = shape \cdot \left[F_{EG}(x)\right]^{shape-1} f_{EG}(x)}

At \code{shape = 1} this is the ex-Gaussian exactly - not approximately - so
\code{\link[=cogmod_exgaussian]{cogmod_exgaussian()}} is nested inside it and \code{loo_compare()} between the two
is like-for-like.
}
\section{What the shape parameter buys}{
A wider range of shapes than the ex-Gaussian can reach. Sweeping \code{sigma} and
\code{tau} across the values RT data occupy, the ex-Gaussian spans skewness 0 to 2
and excess kurtosis 0 to 6; freeing \code{shape} widens that to roughly -0.4 to 4.8
and 0 to 35. In particular the GEG can be \strong{negatively skewed}, which the
ex-Gaussian cannot be at any parameter value.
}

\section{What it costs}{
Interpretability, and specifically the one property that makes the
ex-Gaussian worth using as a \emph{descriptive} model.
\itemize{
\item \strong{The mean is no longer \code{mu + tau}.} With \code{mu = 0.4} and \code{tau = 0.2}, the
mean runs 0.31 at \code{shape = 0.2}, 0.60 at \code{shape = 1} and 1.15 at
\code{shape = 20}. There is no closed form for it - see
\link[brms:posterior_epred.brmsfit]{posterior_epred()} below - and the bulk/tail
decomposition that the ex-Gaussian is normally reported for does not
survive.
\item \strong{\code{shape} is strongly confounded with \code{mu}.} Fitted by maximum likelihood
to the lexical-decision data used in the package vignettes, the correlation
between the two at the optimum is about -0.98, and the other estimates move
with it: on one condition \code{mu} goes 0.429 to 0.508, \code{sigma} 0.051 to 0.037
and \code{tau} 0.119 to 0.162 once \code{shape} is freed. \code{shape} does not add an
independent axis so much as re-slice the same bulk-and-tail split.
}

The practical consequence is that \code{cogmod_priors()} gives \code{shape} a
deliberately informative prior centred on the ex-Gaussian, and that \code{mu},
\code{sigma} and \code{tau} should not be read as the Gaussian centre, the Gaussian SD
and the mean of the tail once \code{shape} is free. If those quantities are the
point of the analysis, fit \code{\link[=cogmod_exgaussian]{cogmod_exgaussian()}} instead. If a better-fitting
descriptive family is the point, \code{\link[=cogmod_logstudent]{cogmod_logstudent()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}} decouple skew from tail weight with parameters that stay
interpretable.
}

\section{Construction}{
The power transform is Durrans' alpha-power (or "exponentiated") family, and
for integer \code{shape} it is the distribution of the maximum of \code{shape}
independent ex-Gaussian draws. That is a mathematical device rather than an
account of a process, so unlike \code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}}'s \code{sigmadrift} there is
no mechanism attached to it.
}

